    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">La</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">casa</span> nueva <span class=\"vp-verb\">está</span> en Valencia.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Noun</strong> (<em>sustantivo</em>): names a person, thing or place: <em>casa, médico, Madrid</em>. It has a gender. <strong>Article</strong> (<em>artículo</em>): comes before a noun: <em>el, la, un, una</em>.",
        "tag": "Noun, article"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Verb</strong> (<em>verbo</em>): says what someone does or what something is: <em>trabajo, comes, es</em>. Its ending changes for the person.",
        "tag": "Verb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Adjective</strong> (<em>adjetivo</em>): describes a noun and matches it: <em>una casa blanca</em>. <strong>Adverb</strong> (<em>adverbio</em>): says how, when or where, and does not change: <em>bien, hoy, aquí, muy</em>.",
        "tag": "Adjective, adverb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Pronoun</strong> (<em>pronombre</em>): stands for a noun: <em>yo, tú, ella</em>. <strong>Preposition</strong> (<em>preposición</em>): a small word before a noun: <em>en, a, de, con, para</em>. <strong>Conjunction</strong> (<em>conjunción</em>): joins words or sentences: <em>y, o, pero, porque</em>.",
        "tag": "Small words"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A quick test: can you put <em>el</em> or <em>la</em> in front of the word? Then it is probably a noun. Does it change with <em>yo, tú, él</em>? Then it is a verb. Does it change with <em>-o, -a</em> next to a noun? Then it is probably an adjective.",
